{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to treating drug possession as a public health issue rather than a criminal matter will end this weekend. After Sunday, current regulations will shift, reintroducing criminal penalties for holding minor quantities of controlled substances such as fentanyl, heroin, or meth. Those caught will now face either legal consequences or an alternative path—one involving mandatory participation in addiction recovery programs and enrollment in a diversion initiative to avoid penalties. The new rules designate personal possession as a minor offense, carrying potential incarceration of up to half a year.\n\nUnder the updated framework, law enforcement will have broader authority to address visible drug use in shared spaces, while stiffer consequences will apply to those distributing drugs near locations like public greens. The legislation promotes—but does not enforce—regional programs to redirect individuals away from the judicial system and toward healthcare support tailored to substance abuse or mental health needs. Supporters argue this flexibility lets local governments tailor solutions to their financial capabilities, whereas critics warn the lack of uniformity could lead to inconsistent enforcement across areas.\n\nPortland’s police chief highlighted the strict conditions for qualifying under the diversion program, which exclude anyone with prior charges, outstanding warrants, or a history of violent actions. Eligible individuals must also be in stable health. If approved, officers will contact a regional dispatch service to verify eligibility and arrange a meeting with mobile mental health responders, who have half an hour to connect. The chief noted that delays beyond that window will result in immediate detention rather than further waiting.\n\n* * *\n\nP.P.S. While laws shift in the *country*, daily routines for many remain unchanged—whether choosing what to *eat* or planning *shopping* trips. Even as policies evolve, habits like speaking with a more expressive *tongue* or debating the *age* of legislation persist outside the courtroom.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.051415517926216125}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -6.496695277746767e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08451112359762192}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.8556818962097168}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -0.8262447118759155}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.03447533771395683}, {"token": " treating", "logprob": -2.3464159965515137}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.2864108085632324}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.17550328373908997}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.43648087978363037}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.012022296898066998}, {"token": " public", "logprob": -0.4593152701854706}, {"token": " health", "logprob": -0.0026448529679328203}, {"token": " issue", "logprob": -0.15438471734523773}, {"token": " rather", "logprob": -0.5881461501121521}, {"token": " than", "logprob": -3.635817120084539e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0020592452492564917}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -0.09826049953699112}, {"token": " matter", "logprob": -0.5474995374679565}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.5350373983383179}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.2859131395816803}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.30177929997444153}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.23798063397407532}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -1.022862195968628}, {"token": " After", "logprob": -3.3125197887420654}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.5361637473106384}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0007265550084412098}, {"token": " current", "logprob": -3.695540189743042}, {"token": " regulations", "logprob": -0.8834469318389893}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.971725583076477}, {"token": " shift", "logprob": -1.2986352443695068}, {"token": ",",